    "title": "What time is the Artemis II splashdown tonight? How to watch live",
    "text": "April 10, 2026, 1:26 p.m. ETNASA's Artemis II mission is set for splashdown in the Pacific Ocean tonight, bringing four astronauts back in a fiery reentry to Earth following a historic trip around the Moon. Here's what to know about what time the Artemis II returns and how to watch Artemis II splashdown and the critical moments Orion blazes a path back home.Space fans can track and watch Artemis II land in the Pacific Ocean in California around 8 p.m. ET tonight ahead of the most critical phase of the Artemis II mission, as the Orion capsule speeds back to Earth at nearly 25,000 mph before slowing for parachute deployment and splashdown. For several tense minutes, all eyes will be on the Orion spacecraft with a known heat shield design flaw as the astronauts face a six-minute communication blackout with Mission Control, placing full trust in Orion's navigation and guidance systems.NASA and US Navy recovery teams are positioned off the California coast, with medical crews ready to assist the astronauts. While some people on land may see the Orion reentry \"fireball,\" the Artemis II splashdown is not visible by beachgoers or coastal residents.What time is Artemis II splashdown tonight?What time is Artemis II splashdown time? NASA expects Artemis II to splash down around 8:07 p.m. ET into the Pacific Ocean off the California coast, though the exact time could shift based on weather, spacecraft navigation adjustments and Orion's reentry. To follow NASA live updates, head to NASA's YouTube Channel for live coverage all evening.How to watch Artemis II splashdown liveTo watch the Artemis II splashdown tonight, Friday, April 10, head to NASA's YouTube channel, where coverage begins at 5:30 p.m. ET or the NASA app. Live streaming and live coverage will also take place on NASA+ and on Amazon Prime. You can also watch on HBO Max, Netflix, and Peacock.Watch Artemis II splashdown on Amazon PrimeWhere will Artemis II splash down?Artemis II's Orion capsule will land in the Pacific Ocean off the coast of Southern California, near San Diego. This location is ideal for NASA and U.S. Navy teams to reach the capsule quickly, assist the astronauts out, and transport them to a nearby recovery ship.The splashdown will occur hundreds of miles offshore, meaning it is not visible from land, though some Californians may be able to see the Orion capsule's \"glowing fireball\" return.Will the NASA astronauts go home after landing?After an initial medical evaluation on a Navy recovery ship, USS John P. Murtha, the four Artemis II astronauts will fly to Houston's Johnson Space Center (JSC), the home of NASA's Mission Control center. The base oversees astronauts before, during and after missions, managing their health, rehabilitation and debriefs.Doctors at JSC specialize in post-spaceflight recovery; astronauts returning from space often experience dizziness and balance issues, blood-pressure shifts and muscle weakness, NASA said.What to expect during Artemis II reentryArtemis II reentry will be the most dangerous part of the mission, with the Orion capsule plunging into Earth's atmosphere at speeds of nearly 25,000 miles per hour, turning the spacecraft into a glowing fireball caused by the superheated air turning into plasma. A six-minute planned communications blackout takes place, where Mission Control cannot hear or see the capsule.Eleven parachutes will be deployed: Artemis II's first parachutes deploy when Orion is traveling around 300 mph, and the final three parachutes slow the capsule from about 130 mph to around 17 mph ahead of splashdown, per NASA.Where is Artemis II right now? Artemis II flight trackerTo follow the Artemis II astronauts in real-time, head to the NASA Artemis II tracker online. Download the free mobile app to track Orion, see how fast Artemis II is going, and how far the astronauts are from the Earth, all in real-time.Who are the NASA astronauts returning on Artemis II?There are three NASA astronauts and one Canadian astronaut on the Artemis II, returning tonight, April 10, shortly after 8 p.m. ET. Here's the crew, their titles, ages and hometowns:Commander Reid Wiseman, 50, of Baltimore, MarylandPilot Victor Glover, 49, of Pomona, CaliforniaMission Specialist Christina Koch, 47, of Grand Rapids, Michigan (raised in North Carolina)Mission Specialist Jeremy Hansen, 50, of London, Ontario, CanadaLori Comstock is a New Jersey-based news reporter covering trending news with USA TODAY Network's Mid-Atlantic Connect Team. She covers news in the Northeast, including New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Delaware, Washington DC, Maryland, and Virginia. Reach her at LComstock@usatodayco.com.",
